3.3.9.m:
the chaff-particles are redundant;
the leftover clarified butter is redundant.
In the redundant, he places the redundant;
moreover, having obtained the redundant by the redundant, he secures it.
The sacrificial bed hid from the gods.
They found it by means of the grass broom.
By the grass broom they found the sacrificial bed, the earth;
she, the earth, spread out earthly things;
she bears the embryo within the creatures;
therefrom the sacrifice is born, all-bestowing.
Before the grass-bush formula, he sweeps the sacrificial bed with the grass broom for finding [it].

Moreover, as there are the grass broom and the sacrificial bed,
[it is] for pair-formation, for procreation.
These are the whiskers of Prajāpati,
namely, the grass broom.
He throws it into the lap of the [sacrificer's] wife;
he makes a pair;
he obtains offspring.
The Hotṛ goes strewing the grass broom from the āhavanīya fire;
thereby he connects the sacrifice up to the next half-month;
he takes hold of it continuous in the next half-month.

3.3.10.m:
He prays for this blessing at the full vessel.
At the end [he does so] with an anuṣṭubh;
this four-footed meter is established for the wife at the full vessel,
thinking: 'May I be established in this world.'
She becomes established in this very world.
Moreover, speech is the anuṣṭubh;
speech is a pair;
the waters are seed, procreative.
From this pair, flashing and thundering, [the cloud] rains,
pouring seed,

3.4.1.m:
For brahman, he seizes a Brahmin;
for regal power, a Rājanya;
for the Maruts, a Vaiśya;
for ascetic fervor, a Śūdra;
for darkness, a thief;
for hell, a slayer of heroes;
for evil, a eunuch;
for buying, an Ayogū;
for desire, a courtesan;
for extreme outcry, a bard from Magadha.

3.4.2.m:
For song, a Sūta;
for dance, an actor;
for dharma, an assembly-goer;
for jest, a chatterer;
for danger, a violent man;
for laughter, a clown;
for bliss, a companion of women;
for delight, the son of an unmarried maiden;
for intelligence, a chariot-maker;
for steadfastness, a carpenter.

3.4.3.m:
For toil, a potter;
for magical craft, a smith;
for beauty, a jewel-maker;
for splendor, an unguent-maker;
for the arrow-shot, an arrow-maker;
for the weapon, a bow-maker;
for action, a bowstring-maker;
for fate, a rope-maker;
for death, a hunter;
for the ender, a dog-keeper.

3.4.4.m:
For union, a paramour;
for the home, a second husband;
for ruin, a younger brother married before the elder;
for distress, an elder brother unmarried after the younger;
for failure, the husband of an elder sister whose younger sister has married first;
for purification, a physician;
for wisdom, an astrologer;
for adornment, an embroideress;
for strength, an oblation-bearer;
for color, a tracker.

3.4.5.m:
For rivers, a Pauñjiṣṭha;
for bears, a Niṣāda;
for the man-tiger, a reckless brawler;
for madnesses, a madman;
for Gandharvas and Apsarases, a Vrātya;
for serpent-deities, one who cannot pace in meter;
for the Avī deities, a gambler;
for wanderlust, a non-gambler;
for Piśācas, a basket-weaver;
for Yātudhānas, a maker of thorns.

3.4.6.m:
For destructions, a hunchback;
for delight, a dwarf;
for door-posts, a lame man;
for sleep, a blind man;
for unrighteousness, a deaf man;
for concord, an inciter of love;
for arbitrary speech, an attendant;
for study, an interrogator;
for apprentice study, a cross-examiner;
for boundaries, an arbitrator of questions.

3.4.7.m:
For strife, a thief at heart;
for manslaughter, an informer;
for discrimination, a distributor;
for oversight, a charioteer;
for strength, a follower;
for abundance, an attendant;
for the dear, a speaker of pleasant words;
for security, a horse-rider;
for sacrifice, a washerwoman of clothes;
for desire, a woman dyer.

3.4.8.m:
For light, a wood-gatherer;
for radiance, a fire-kindler;
for the back of the firmament, a consecrator;
for the summit of the sun, a vessel-cleanser;
for the world of the gods, a carver;
for the world of men, a scatterer;
for all the worlds, a sprinkler;
for distress and slaughter, a churner;
for the heavenly realm, a distributor of shares;
for the highest firmament, a server of meals.

3.4.9.m:
For ruins, an elephant-keeper;
for speed, a horse-keeper;
for nourishment, a cowherd;
for fiery energy, a goat-herd;
for heroic power, a shepherd;
for food, a cultivator;
for sweet drink, a liquor-maker;
for good fortune, a house-guard;
for welfare, a guardian of wealth;
for superintendence, an assistant distributor.

3.4.10.m:
For wrath, a metal-smelter;
for anger, a pursuer;
for grief, an attacker;
for steep slope and descent, a three-legged cripple;
for exertion, a yoker;
for rest, an unyoker;
for physical form, an artist;
for virtue, an ointment-maker;
for ruin, an embroideress;
for Yama, a barren woman.

3.4.11.m:
For Yamī, a mother of twins;
for the Atharvans, a woman who has miscarried;
for the year, a wandering woman;
for the Parivatsara, a woman who has not given birth;
for the Idāvatsara, a woman who leaps over boundaries;
for the Idvatsara, a woman who transgresses;
for the Vatsara, a decrepit woman;
for the Sarvaṃtsara, a gray-haired woman;
for the forest, a forest-guard;
for the wilderness beyond, a wildfire-warden.

3.4.12.m:
For lakes, a fisherman;
for ponds, a boatman;
for stagnant pools, a Bainda;
for reed-beds, a dried-fish dealer;
for the near bank, a Kaivarta;
for the far bank, a Mārgāra;
for fords, an Ānda;
for uneven places, a Maināla;
for sounds, a Parṇaka;
for caves, a Kirāta;
for mountain ridges, a Jambhaka;
for mountains, a Kiṃpuruṣa.

3.4.13.m:
For the echo, an imitator;
for noise, a barking dog-keeper;
for the end, a babbler;
for the endless, a mute;
for greatness, a lute-player;
for clamor, a flute-blower;
for wailing, a drum-beater;
for all around, a conch-blower;
for the Ṛbhus, a skin-stitcher;
for the Sādhyas, a leather-worker.

3.4.14.m:
For loathsomeness, a Paulkasa;
for prosperity, a wakeful man;
for misfortune, a sleeper;
for the balance, a merchant;
for color, a goldsmith;
for the All-gods, a leprous man;
for subsequent guilt, a dullard;
for strife, a slanderer;
for ruinous failure, an insolent man;
for destruction, a wood-cutter.

3.4.15.m:
For laughter, he seizes a courtesan;
a lute-player and an astrologer for song;
for sea-monsters, a speckled woman;
for jest, a gracious woman;
a flute-blower, a headman, and a hand-clapper for dance;
for joy, a comforter;
for bliss, a clapper.

3.4.16.m:
For the King of Dice, a gambler;
for the Kṛta age, a frequenter of assemblies;
for the Tretā age, an observer of vices;
for the Dvāpara age, an outsider;
for the Kali age, a permanent fixture of the assembly;
for evil deed, a roving teacher;
for the road, a Vedic student;
for Piśācas, a stone-cleaver;
for thirst, a cow-tormentor;
for ruin, a cow-slayer;
for hunger, a cow-carver;
for hunger and thirst, that one
who approaches begging for meat while one carves up an ox.

3.4.17.m:
For the earth, he seizes a cripple who moves on a stool;
for Agni, a brawny man;
for Vāyu, a Cāṇḍāla;
for the intermediate space, a bamboo-dancer;
for heaven, a bald man;
for the sun, a yellow-eyed man;
for the moon, a blinking man;
for the lunar mansions, a spotted man;
for the day, a fair man with reddish-brown eyes;
for the night, a dark man with reddish-brown eyes.

3.4.18.m:
For speech, he seizes a man;
exhalation, inhalation, diffused breath, upward breath, and concentrated breath—these for Vāyu;
for the sun, he seizes the eye;
the mind for the moon;
for the directions, the ear;
for Prajāpati, a man.

3.5.2.m:
Forward [go] your prizes, shining every day,
accompanied by oblations, with the [ladle] flowing with ghee;
seeking favor, he goes to the gods.
O Agni, come to the feast,
being praised, to the giving of oblation;
sit down as Hotṛ upon the strew.
As such, O Aṅgiras, with firesticks
and with ghee we magnify you;
glow greatly, O youngest one!
May he [bring] us broad, renowned [wealth].

3.5.7.m:
Agni is the head, the summit of heaven,
this lord of the earth;
he quickens the seeds of the waters.
You became the leader of the sacrifice and of the atmospheric realm,
where you attend with your propitious span of steeds.
You placed your head in heaven, winning the sun;
you made your tongue the bearer of oblations, O Agni.
O Prajāpati, none other than you
has encompassed all these beings born;
with whatever desire we sacrifice to you, let that be ours.

May we be lords of riches!
He knows the father, he the mother, as the son;
he shall become the son, he shall become repeatedly giving gifts;
he covered the sky, the intermediate space, and the light of heaven;
he became all existences, he came into being.
O Agni and Soma, of one accord,
accept our songs with one invocation;
you came into unison among the gods.
You placed these lights in heaven,
Agni and Soma, of one resolve.

3.7.2.m:
He should press it down with the bottom of the sruva spoon:
'Do not faint; let not the sacrifice faint; let not the sacrificer faint.
Homage be to you coming;
homage, O Rudra, to you departing;
homage to where you sit down;
do not harm so-and-so, do not harm so-and-so'—referring to the one towards whom it jumped.
He should cast it [into the fire]:
'The thousand-horned bull, Jātavedas,
backed by chants, rich in ghee, fair-faced,
may he not forsake us, when addressed let him not forsake us;
bestow on us increase of cattle and increase of heroes.'
With the brahman he casts it.
That is the expiation for it.

3.7.3.m:
He says: "Agni";
Agni, indeed, is the placer of seed;
he places seed indeed.
He says: "Indra";
he places manly power into him.
He says: "Tvaṣṭṛ";
Tvaṣṭṛ, indeed, is the fashioner of forms for pairs of domestic animals;
he places form in the domestic animals.
He says: "Bṛhaspati";
Bṛhaspati, indeed, is the brahman of the gods;
by the brahman he generates offspring for him.
He says: "It has dripped down onto the earth";
he establishes it in this [earth]. He says: "It does not reach Nirṛti turning away";
[it is] for the warding off of demons.

3.7.4.m:
May Agni, carrier of oblations, bring them here;
this full-moon oblation of theirs is in me;
this new-moon oblation of theirs is in me.
Between the two fires, the domestic animals
have come to the assembly of the gods;
those I grasp first
in [their] own abode with thought.
May all-formed offspring rest here,
dwelling around Agni, the master of the house;
those I grasp first

in [their] own abode with thought.
May all-formed domestic animals rest here,
dwelling around Agni, the master of the house;
those I grasp first
in [their] own abode with thought.
This fire of the ancestors
has carried the oblations to the ancestors;
that [fire] I grasp first;
may it make our food free from poison.
May the guardians of the hall continuously

who have Indra as their chief.
Here I announce the sacrifice to the Ādityas,
who have Varuṇa as their chief.
Rich in milk are the plants;
rich in milk is the sap of the herbs;
that sap which is the milk of the waters—
with that, O Indra, unite me.
O Agni, lord of vows, I will perform the vow;
may I be able to do that; may that succeed for me.
O Vāyu, lord of vows! O Āditya, lord of vows!

3.7.6.m:
subordinate to the dhruvā spoon is this intention of his.
May I be superior;
may my rivals be beneath me.
You are the mighty bull,
son of the butter-flowing [ladles].
Sit in your dear seat with your dear name;
sit kindly for me, easy to sit upon on the earth.
Extend [me] with offspring, with domestic animals, in the heavenly realm;
sit in heaven, on earth, in the intermediate space.
May I be superior;

may my rivals be beneath me.
This pot is full of ghee,
a fountain of a hundred streams with unceasing milk.
With divine shelter through the Maruts,
you are the sacrifice, supported on every side;
may what has been and what will be support me on every side.
May a hundred blessings be mine;
may a thousand pleasant words be mine,
full of refreshments, full of domestic animals.
You are Prajāpati, supported on every side;

may what has been and what will be support me on every side.
May a hundred blessings be mine;
may a thousand pleasant words be mine,
full of refreshments, full of domestic animals.
This is the immortal, potent heroic power;
by this, the domestic animals were discerned for Indra.
Thereby, O gods, favor me.
Here may I win refreshment, vigor, glory, victorious power, and splendorous might.
May what is cooked rest in me;
that which moved over the earth has entered it.

Wherewith Prajāpati poured strength into Indra,
this is that bright, prize-winning sweetness;
wherewith he pleased Mahendra from above,
may the curds please me.
This sacred broom found the earth,
which was hidden in the deep, in recesses;
may it find a world for the sacrificer;
may it of manifold actions make the sacrifice free from defect.
This sacrifice, possessing oblations, has taken its place,
with the Ṛg verse, the Sāman, the Yajus, and with the deities.

for winning the prize I wipe down;
Agni, the eater of food, for the eating of food.
Invoked is Father Sky;
may Father Sky invoke me;
Agni from the Agnīdhra fire,
for lifespan, spiritual preeminence, life-sustenance, and holiness.
Invoked is Mother Earth;
may Mother Earth invoke me;
Agni from the Agnīdhra fire,

for lifespan, spiritual preeminence,
for life-sustenance, and holiness.
May mind, the light, relish the clarified butter;
may it put together this broken sacrifice;
may Bṛhaspati extend this [sacrifice] of ours;
may the All-gods rejoice here.
Whomever, O Agni, I cut off,
or if I, being injured, wander about,
both his offspring and his root
do you, O gods, cut off downward.

A peg of heaven driven down,
arisen from the earth,
with that of a thousand joints
we cause the hater to grieve.
May my hater grieve much;
O herb, may I not grieve.
O sacrifice, homage to thee, O sacrifice;
homage and homage to thee, O sacrifice.
Conclude for me propitiously;
conclude for me kindly;

conclude for me with prosperity;
conclude for me with spiritual preeminence;
conclude following the success of the sacrifice.
Near to thee, O sacrifice, is homage;
near to thee is homage;
near to thee is homage.
Of [the grains] being husked three times,
whatever remainder is left behind,
the portion of the rakṣases—
may the waters carry that away from here.

Whatever has stuck in the mortar, in the pestle, and on the winnowing basket,
what on the millstone, what on the potsherd,
the falling drops, the scattered particles, I offer together;
may the All-gods relish this oblation.
Whatever many drops there are in the sacrifice,
all those I offer in the fire, well-sacrificed, well-poured.
Rising today, O Mitra of vast might,
destroy my rivals;
strike them with lightning from heaven;
setting, make them low.

Rising today, share out to us
as a father to his sons;
for thou rulest over long life,
grant us that, O Sūrya.
Rising today, O Mitra of vast might,
mounting the higher heaven,
my heart disease, O Sūrya,
and the jaundice make vanish.
My jaundice to the parrots
and to the starlings we transfer;

3.8.1.m:
He sacrifices with the Sāṃgrahaṇī offering,
[thinking:] ‘May I comprehend this community.’
The halter is twelve cubits long;
the year has twelve months;
he secures the year itself.
It is made of muñja-grass;
muñja-grass is vigor;
he secures vigor itself.
Citrā is the lunar mansion;
wonderful indeed is this rite:

namely, the horse sacrifice, for success.
He settles upon a place of divine worship with an auspicious name;
thereby he wins auspicious renown.
They bring along the priests, except those on foot, up to the Subrahmaṇyā chant,
for the attainment of the heavenly realm.
He shaves his hair and beard;
he trims his nails;
he cleans his teeth;
he bathes;
he puts on an unwashed garment,
for the removal of evil.
Restraining his speech, he fasts,
for the guarding of the heavenly realm.
They sit keeping watch through the night,
for the attainment of the heavenly realm.
